speechtext:
Item agreed to.
Other projects-to provide for reimbursement to the Canadian Pacific Railway Company and to the Canadian National Railway Company, upon such terms and conditions as the governor in council may determine, of expenditures to be made by each of the said companies for:
(a)	Labour in the employment of certain unemployment relief forces on special work in extension of the said companies' programs of maintenance of way and betterment works during the year 1936;
(b)	Workmen's compensation liability relative to such labour;
(c)	Certain allowances for transportation of such labour;
[Mr. Maclnnis.l
(d)	Certain other expenditures for labour, material and overhead incurred by the railway companies if such expenditures are made during 1936 in preparation for or in completion of work expected to be performed by the unemployment relief forces but not performed because of shortage of unemployment relief forces, not exceeding in the aggregate, $3,100,000.

The item we are dealing with is 419, which reads:
To provide for reimbursement to the Canadian Pacific Railway Company and to the Canadian National Railway Company, upon such terms and conditions as the governor in council may determine, of expenditures to be made by each of the said companies for:
(a)	Labour in the employment of certain unemployment relief forces on special work in extension of the said companies' programs of maintenance of way and betterment works during the year 1936;
(b)	Workmen's compensation liability relative to such labour;
(c)	Certain allowances for transportation of such labour;
(d)	Certain other expenditures for labour, material and overhead incurred by the railway companies if such expenditures are made during 1936 in preparation for or in completion of work expected to be performed by the unemployment relief forces but not performed because of shortage of unemployment relief forces, not exceeding in the aggregate. $3,100,000.

Supply-Railways-Relief Works
Now I wish to ask a question and not to make a statement, and in that respect I do not think I shall be infringing the rule. In discussing an item which has to do with expenditures on maintenance of way, and other purposes to be determined by the governor in council, is it or is it not competent for me or any other hon. member to turn to the sheet in the company's annual report in which this very question is dealt with, wherein they appropriate certain moneys themselves, and to compare their program with what we are to give them, in order to ascertain whether they need more or less than is being provided? If I am to make comparisons of this sort it is necessary for me to look at the Canadian Pacific Railway Company's annual report, which has come into my hands. If I am told, in discussing this item, that I may not refer to that report in urging the Minister of Railways to exercise the discretion that remains in the governor in council, by order in council, to do certain things; if I may not, in my own feeble and humble way, offer what I regard as sound reasons why perhaps the matter should be reconsidered, but am told that I am out of order, then I respect the ruling of the chair. I respect the hon. gentleman who is in the chair, the Deputy Speaker and Chairman of this committee, and I shall always be prepared to bow to his ruling. But I ask the chairman now whether I am out of order when I use this report to assist me in giving my reasons why some change should be made in this particular vote.
